{"file":"parenting-frameworks-0.2.0-v2.zip","bytes":117008,"status":"已读取","plugins":[{"path":".claude-plugin/plugin.json","name":"parenting-frameworks","description":"Parenting & Education Framework Guide — meta-router for 16 frameworks (Positive Discipline, PET, CPS, Emotion Coaching, Triple P, Attachment Parenting, SDT, Retrieval Practice, Growth Mindset, UbD, PBL, Montessori, DAP, UDL, Formative Assessment, Differentiated Instruction). Invoke /parenting.","version":"0.2.0","license":"MIT","repository":"https://github.com/ironyjk/parenting-frameworks","homepage":"https://github.com/ironyjk/parenting-frameworks","keywords":["parenting","education","child-development","montessori","pbl","korean"]}],"skills":[{"path":"attachment-parenting/SKILL.md","name":"attachment-parenting","description":"Attachment-Based Parenting (Bowlby, Ainsworth, Siegel) — Build secure attachment through sensitive responsiveness, the 4 S's, repair conversations, and the Circle of Security","headings":["Attachment-Based Parenting Skill","Sub-commands","The 4 S's in Action","Repair: The Most Important Attachment Skill","Repair Conversation Protocol","What Repair Teaches","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Attachment-Based Analysis","Attachment Dynamics","Sensitive Response","If Rupture Occurred","Example Dialogue Script","Reflection for Parent"],"name_hint":"attachment · parenting"},{"path":"cps/SKILL.md","name":"cps","description":"Collaborative & Proactive Solutions (Ross Greene) — 'Kids do well if they can.' Solve problems WITH children, not TO them. Plan B collaborative process.","headings":["Collaborative & Proactive Solutions (CPS) Skill","Sub-commands","Plan B: The Three Steps","Step 1: Empathy — Gather Information","Step 2: Define the Adult's Concern","Step 3: The Invitation — Collaborate","Common Failures and Fixes","\"I Don't Know\"","Solution Falls Apart","Emergency Plan B (in the moment)","Slipping into Plan A","Anti-patterns","Output Format","CPS Analysis","ALSUP Assessment","Plan Selection","Plan B Dialogue Script","Solution Test"],"name_hint":"cps"},{"path":"dap/SKILL.md","name":"dap","description":"Developmentally Appropriate Practice (NAEYC) — a framework for early childhood education that promotes optimal learning and development by meeting children where they are, accounting for age, individual, and cultural factors.","headings":["Developmentally Appropriate Practice (DAP) Skill","DAP Decision-Making Framework","Quick Reference: Age-Appropriate Expectations","0-3 Years","3-5 Years","5-8 Years","Anti-patterns","Output Format","DAP Analysis","Three-Lens Assessment","Developmental Considerations","Recommended Approach","Play-Based Alternatives","Family Partnership"],"name_hint":"dap"},{"path":"differentiated-instruction/SKILL.md","name":"differentiated-instruction","description":"Differentiated Instruction (Carol Ann Tomlinson) — a proactive approach to teaching that adjusts content, process, product, and learning environment based on students' readiness, interest, and learning profile to maximize growth for every learner.","headings":["Differentiated Instruction Skill","Execution Protocol","1. Clarify KUD (same for ALL students)","2. Pre-Assess","3. Differentiate by Element + Characteristic","4. Apply Strategies","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Differentiated Instruction Plan","KUD (Same for All)","Pre-Assessment Strategy","Differentiation Plan","Grouping Strategy","Anchor Activities"],"name_hint":"differentiated · instruction"},{"path":"emotion-coaching/SKILL.md","name":"emotion-coaching","description":"Emotion Coaching (John Gottman) — Raise emotionally intelligent children through 5 steps: awareness, connection, empathic listening, naming emotions, and problem-solving with limits","headings":["Emotion Coaching Skill","Sub-commands","The Five Steps of Emotion Coaching","Step 1: Be Aware of the Child's Emotion","Step 2: Recognize as Opportunity for Connection","Step 3: Listen Empathically and Validate","Step 4: Help Name the Emotion","Step 5: Set Limits and Help Problem-Solve","Full Example: Tantrum at Store (Age 5)","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Emotion Coaching Analysis","Current Style Assessment","5-Step Emotion Coaching Response","Example Dialogue Script","Emotion Vocabulary Expansion"],"name_hint":"emotion · coaching"},{"path":"formative-assessment/SKILL.md","name":"formative-assessment","description":"Formative Assessment (Black & Wiliam) — assessment FOR learning, not just OF learning. Five key strategies and dozens of practical techniques to make student thinking visible and adjust instruction in real time.","headings":["Formative Assessment Skill","Execution Protocol","1. Clarify the Target","2. Elicit Evidence","3. Interpret the Evidence","4. Act on the Evidence","5. Provide Feedback That Moves Forward","6. Activate Peer and Self-Assessment","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Formative Assessment Plan","Learning Intention and Success Criteria","Evidence Collection Plan","Feedback Strategy","Peer/Self-Assessment"],"name_hint":"formative · assessment"},{"path":"growth-mindset/SKILL.md","name":"growth-mindset","description":"Growth Mindset (Carol Dweck) — the belief that abilities can be developed through dedication and hard work. Transform praise, feedback, and self-talk to build resilience and love of learning.","headings":["Growth Mindset Skill","Execution Protocol","1. Identify the Mindset Trigger","2. Respond with Growth Reframe","3. Use Process Praise (Effort + Strategy + Progress)","4. Teach About the Brain","5. For Teachers","Growth Mindset Self-Assessment","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Growth Mindset Analysis","Mindset Assessment","Growth Reframe","Example Dialogue Script","Watch For"],"name_hint":"growth · mindset"},{"path":"montessori/SKILL.md","name":"montessori","description":"Montessori Method (Maria Montessori) — child-led education through prepared environments, sensory materials, mixed-age grouping, and respect for natural developmental rhythms. Over 100 years of practice across 20,000+ schools worldwide.","headings":["Montessori Method Skill","Execution Protocol","1. Identify the Sensitive Period","2. Assess the Environment","3. Observe Before Acting","4. Connect Child to Environment","5. Step Back","Quick Reference: Curriculum Areas and Key Materials","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Montessori Analysis","Sensitive Period Assessment","Environment Check","Recommended Materials/Activities","Guide's Role"],"name_hint":"montessori"},{"path":"parenting/SKILL.md","name":"parenting","description":"Parenting & Education Framework Guide — Analyzes age, role (parent/teacher), and situation to auto-select the optimal combination from 16 parenting and education frameworks. Supports Positive Discipline, PET, CPS, Emotion Coaching, Triple P, Attachment Parenting, SDT, Retrieval Practice, Growth Mindset, UbD, PBL, Montessori, DAP, UDL, Formative Assessment, and Differentiated Instruction.","headings":["Parenting — Education & Parenting Framework Meta-Router","Input Schema","The 16 Frameworks — Quick Reference","Detection Matrix","Age-Based Routing","Role-Based Routing","Emotion Level Routing","Decision Flowchart","Fallback Strategy — Universal Parenting Sequence","Execution Strategy","Step 1: Listen and Classify","Step 2: Select Framework(s)","Step 3: Execute","Sequential (dependent pipeline)","then after output:","Parallel (independent analysis)","Step 4: Synthesize","Sub-Commands"],"name_hint":"parenting"},{"path":"pbl/SKILL.md","name":"pbl","description":"Project-Based Learning (PBL) — students gain knowledge and skills by working for an extended period to investigate and respond to an authentic, engaging, and complex question, problem, or challenge.","headings":["Project-Based Learning (PBL) Skill","Execution Protocol","1. Design the Driving Question","2. Plan the Project (7 Essential Elements Checklist)","3. Scaffold the Process","4. Formative Checkpoints","5. Assess Both Process and Product","Anti-patterns","Output Format","PBL Project Design","Driving Question","7 Elements Check","Scaffolding Plan","Assessment Plan"],"name_hint":"pbl"},{"path":"pet/SKILL.md","name":"pet","description":"Parent Effectiveness Training (Thomas Gordon) — Problem ownership, Active Listening, I-Messages, and the No-Lose Method for resolving parent-child conflicts","headings":["Parent Effectiveness Training (P.E.T.) Skill","Sub-commands","Problem Ownership Decision Tree","Tool 1: Active Listening (Child Owns the Problem)","Technique","Example: Child (age 8) comes home upset","Tool 2: I-Messages (Parent Owns the Problem)","Three-Part Formula","The Shift — I-Message to Active Listening","Tool 3: The No-Lose Method (Method III)","Six Steps","Example: Bedtime conflict (age 7)","Anti-patterns","Output Format","P.E.T. Analysis","Problem Ownership","Recommended Approach","Example Dialogue Script"],"name_hint":"pet"},{"path":"positive-discipline/SKILL.md","name":"positive-discipline","description":"Positive Discipline (Jane Nelsen) — Adlerian psychology-based parenting that is Kind AND Firm simultaneously. Belonging + Significance as core needs.","headings":["Positive Discipline Skill","Sub-commands","Execution Protocol","1. Identify the Mistaken Goal","2. Curiosity Questions (instead of lectures)","3. Logical Consequences (4 R's: Related, Respectful, Reasonable, Revealed)","4. Family Meetings (weekly, 15-30 min)","5. Positive Time-Out (Cool-Down Space)","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Positive Discipline Analysis","Mistaken Goal Identification","Kind AND Firm Response","Recommended PD Tools","Example Dialogue Script"],"name_hint":"positive · discipline"},{"path":"retrieval-practice/SKILL.md","name":"retrieval-practice","description":"Retrieval Practice + Spaced Repetition — strengthen long-term memory by pulling information out of your brain, not pushing it back in. The testing effect is one of the most robust findings in cognitive science.","headings":["Retrieval Practice + Spaced Repetition Skill","Execution Protocol","For Educators","For Self-Learners","For Parents (Study Coaching)","Key Techniques Quick Reference","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Retrieval Practice Plan","Current Study Habits Assessment","Retrieval Strategy","Spacing Schedule","Quick Wins"],"name_hint":"retrieval · practice"},{"path":"sdt-parenting/SKILL.md","name":"sdt-parenting","description":"SDT Parenting (Deci & Ryan) — Support children's autonomy, competence, and relatedness. Autonomy-supportive vs. controlling parenting. Why rewards can backfire.","headings":["SDT Parenting (Self-Determination Theory) Skill","Sub-commands","Autonomy-Supportive Parenting: Four Elements","1. Acknowledge the Child's Perspective","2. Provide Meaningful Rationale","3. Offer Choices Within Limits","4. Minimize Pressure and Control","Application to Common Challenges","Homework","Screen Time","Chores","Eating","What to Do Instead of Rewards","Anti-patterns","Output Format","SDT Parenting Analysis","Basic Needs Assessment","Motivation Type"],"name_hint":"sdt · parenting"},{"path":"triple-p/SKILL.md","name":"triple-p","description":"Triple P Positive Parenting Program (Matthew Sanders) — Evidence-based 5-level system from universal prevention to intensive family intervention. Self-regulation framework for parents and children.","headings":["Triple P Positive Parenting Program Skill","Sub-commands","Key Strategies","Descriptive Praise","Planned Activities","Assertive Discipline (Graduated Response)","Quiet Time and Time-Out","Behavior Charts","Anti-patterns","Output Format","Triple P Analysis","Intervention Level","Relevant Principles","Recommended Strategies","Example Dialogue Script","Parent Self-Regulation Check"],"name_hint":"triple · p"},{"path":"ubd/SKILL.md","name":"ubd","description":"Understanding by Design (Wiggins & McTighe) — backward design framework that starts with desired results, determines acceptable evidence, then plans learning experiences. The gold standard for curriculum design.","headings":["Understanding by Design (